---
import CounterIllustration from "../../components/CounterIllustration.astro";
import SharedCounterDemo from "../../components/SharedCounterDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import StructureLessonLayout from "../../components/StructureLessonLayout.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import {
  sequencerTerm,
  sharedCounterTerm,
  sluiceTerm,
} from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
---

<StructureLessonLayout
  title="SharedCounter"
  description="A ranger sequencer numbers signed bird-count changes and broadcasts each operation to Alice, Bob, and Carol."
  articleClass="structure-page"
  illustrationId="structures-shared-counter"
>
    <Fragment slot="summary">
      Alice, Bob, and Carol still need to combine new sightings with
      corrections. This time, they send each change to a ranger who keeps one
      numbered station log.
    </Fragment>

    <StructureFacts
      structure="SharedCounter"
      family="DDS"
      model="Sequenced operation-based distributed data structure"
      updates="Signed integer deltas"
      merge="Apply each sequencer-assigned operation once, in sequence order."
      delivery="Repeated network delivery is safe when replicas reject a sequence number they have applied."
      bestFor="Shared totals under an ordered collaborative runtime"
      cost="One confirmed integer plus local pending operations"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="ranger-log-title">
      <h2 id="ranger-log-title">The ranger opens a numbered log</h2>
      <p>
        The hikers stop exchanging cumulative notebook pages. Each hiker now
        sends a signed change to the ranger station. The ranger writes the
        change on the next numbered line and sends a copy to all three hikers.
      </p>
      <p>
        That ranger is a{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#sequencer">sequencer</a></dfn>: a service that
        gives accepted operations one shared order.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...sequencerTerm} />
      <p>
        For Alice and Bob's race, the ranger's ledger has two lines:
      </p>
      <table aria-label="Ranger's numbered SharedCounter ledger">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Sequence</th>
            <th scope="col">Note from</th>
            <th scope="col">Signed change</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">SN 1</th><td>Alice</td><td>+3</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">SN 2</th><td>Bob</td><td>-1</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The ranger broadcasts each line after he writes it. Carol misses{" "}
        <code>SN 1</code>, but then receives <code>SN 2</code>. Her notebook
        shows a gap in the sequence, so she knows that a sequence number is
        missing. She holds <code>SN 2</code> and asks the ranger to resend the
        missing ledger line. She applies Alice's <code>+3</code> from{" "}
        <code>SN 1</code>, then Bob's <code>-1</code> from <code>SN 2</code>.
      </p>
      <p>The sequencer has three jobs in this story:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Assign a number.</strong> Stamp each accepted operation with the next sequence number.</li>
        <li><strong>Broadcast the operation.</strong> Send the same numbered stream to every replica.</li>
        <li><strong>Support recovery.</strong> A runtime can store operations or summaries for replicas that reconnect later.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Carol can request <code>SN 1</code> only if the ranger or the runtime
        retains the numbered operations. A production runtime can send a
        current snapshot instead of replaying old ledger lines. The
        single-browser demo delivers every numbered copy. In three-device
        mode, the browser holds later operations and requests missing lines
        from the stored log.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CounterIllustration kind="shared-counter" />

    <section aria-labelledby="delivery-title">
      <h2 id="delivery-title">One number means one application</h2>
      <p>
        A sequence number does not force the network to deliver one copy.
        The network can still deliver a numbered copy twice. The number gives each replica a
        stable operation identity: apply a new number, and ignore a number that
        has already been applied.
      </p>
      <p>
        The numbers also show relative order. If <code>SN 2</code> appears
        before <code>SN 1</code>, the replica can detect a gap instead of
        losing an operation without warning. The sequence number exposes the
        gap. Carol must ask the ranger to resend the missing operation, or
        the runtime must supply a current snapshot.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="shared-race-title">
      <h2 id="shared-race-title">The ranger sequences two notes</h2>
      <p>
        The group starts from the agreed count of 10. Alice counts 3 new birds.
        Bob finds one duplicate sighting. They send <code>+3</code> and
        <code>-1</code> before either sees the other's change.
      </p>
      <table aria-label="Bird count as SharedCounter operations are sequenced">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Moment</th>
            <th scope="col">Alice</th>
            <th scope="col">Bob</th>
            <th scope="col">Carol</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Agreed count</th><td>10</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Notes in transit</th><td>13</td><td>9</td><td>10</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">SN 1 and SN 2 applied</th><td>12</td><td>12</td><td>12</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        A{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#sharedcounter">SharedCounter</a></dfn> stores
        one integer and sends signed operations through this sequenced path.
        The local update is optimistic. The numbered broadcast confirms it.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...sharedCounterTerm} />
    </section>

    <SharedCounterDemo />

    <section aria-labelledby="live-room-title">
      <h2 id="live-room-title">The real sequencer and storage</h2>
      <p>
        Create a room and open its link on three devices. The server assigns
        Alice, Bob, and Carol to the first three connections. Further devices
        can observe. Each browser keeps three Watershed counter-kernel states,
        so you can compare your hiker's optimistic value with the other copies.
        Each device can send changes only for its assigned hiker.
      </p>
      <p>
        When Alice clicks <code>+3</code>, her browser calls the kernel's
        increment operation immediately. It sends the signed amount and a
        unique action ID to one Cloudflare{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#durable-object">Durable Object</a></dfn>.
        This service receives the room's requests, assigns consecutive sequence numbers,
        and stores each operation in SQLite before broadcasting it. The
        operation includes its action ID, author, signed amount, and sequence
        number. The server does not broadcast cumulative counter values.
      </p>
      <TermCallout
        term="Durable Object"
        definition="A Cloudflare service that combines coordination for one named object with storage private to that object."
      />
      <p>
        A <dfn><a href="/glossary/#websocket">WebSocket</a></dfn> keeps the
        browser's connection open so the server can send numbered operations
        without waiting for another browser request.
      </p>
      <TermCallout
        term="WebSocket"
        definition="A persistent connection over which a browser and a server can send messages to each other."
      />
      <p>
        Alice's browser matches its pending edit with the numbered echo and
        calls the kernel's acknowledgement operation. Her count does not
        increase twice. Other copies apply the signed amount through the
        kernel's remote-operation path. The browser transport ignores
        repeated sequence numbers. If a later number arrives first, it holds
        that operation and requests the missing prefix before applying it.
      </p>
      <p>
        SQLite retains up to 1,000 operations per room. Rejoining starts the
        kernels from the agreed count of 10 and replays the stored log in
        sequence order. Reset clears the log, restores 10, and creates a new
        room epoch. The server rejects requests from the old epoch. An
        operation rejected by the server causes the browser to roll back
        pending edits and reload the stored log.
      </p>
      <p>
        Broadcast, Speed, and Jitter control the local delivery display.
        They do not delay the real Durable Object's sequencing or storage.
        There is no persistent offline outbox or automatic reconnect. If
        the connection closes, controls stop accepting edits and values may
        still include unconfirmed changes. Rejoin to recover the stored log.
        Room roles belong to connections, not authenticated identities.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="comparison-title">
      <h2 id="comparison-title">The number moved; the safety mechanism changed</h2>
      <p>
        All three counters have a value of 12, but they do not exchange the same
        information. The G-counter sends cumulative positive components. The
        PN-counter sends cumulative positive and negative components. The
        SharedCounter sends signed operations with sequence numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Signed addition gives the same final sum in either order. The sequencer
        still matters because a raw <code>+3</code> must be distinguished from
        a second delivery of the same <code>+3</code>. Other sequenced data
        structures also use the shared order when operation order changes the
        result.
      </p>
      <p>
        This design depends on the sequencer. If that service is unavailable,
        replicas can keep optimistic local work, but they cannot confirm a new
        shared order until sequencing resumes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <h2 id="notes-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Watershed's public{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed.gleam#L1626-L1674">SharedCounter API</a>
        {" "}and{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/counter_kernel.gleam">counter kernel</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Implementation:</strong> The single-browser mode calls the API
        through the Gleam toolkit and connects three documents through{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#sluice">Sluice</a></dfn>. The real room calls the same
        counter kernel through a typed toolkit boundary. A small Durable Object
        Sluice facade shares WebSocket connections, hiker assignment, and
        broadcasts with the G-counter rooms. SharedCounter-specific code
        stores and replays numbered signed operations; it does not implement
        Watershed's full document transport.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...sluiceTerm} />
    </section>

</StructureLessonLayout>

<style>
  pre {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    overflow-x: auto;
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--clear) 45%, var(--sky-sheet));
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    margin-block: var(--space-5);
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  :is(th, td) {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  th {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
  }

  td {
    font-family: var(--font-data);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

</style>
